1 VMINIT
    The VMINIT command creates a functional virtual memory map that uses
    physical memory to create the P0, P1, and S0 virtual address regions
    and the privileged modes' stacks.  It also initializes the required
    privileged registers and stack pointers to begin using it.  The command
    displays the physical and virtual address ranges of each region it
    creates.  INITIALIZE/VAX must come first.  The startup procedure,
    STARTUP.COM, runs it with sizes the configuration may set (see HELP
    CONFIG KEYS, vax.memory.*).

    Syntax:

        VMINIT [/P0=pages] [/P1=pages] [/S0=pages]
               [/KSP=pages] [/ESP=pages] [/SSP=pages] [/ISP=pages]
               [/STRINGPOOL=pages] [/[NO]PAGING]

    Each value is a count of 512-byte pages, a decimal integer (the
    console's radix doesn't apply).

    Qualifiers:

        /P0=pages       The pages of the P0 region, the program region.
        /P1=pages       The pages of the P1 region, the control region.
        /S0=pages       The pages of the S0 region, system space.

                        With paging off, a region left out (or given 0)
                        shares, with the others left out, whatever
                        physical memory the regions given sizes leave,
                        and any physical memory still left over is added
                        to S0.  P0 and P1 are paged on demand, so they may
                        together be larger than physical memory; a
                        program can still exhaust physical memory by
                        touching too many pages, since there is no paging
                        to backing store.  All of S0 is mapped to
                        physical memory, so it can be no larger.

                        With paging on, the regions cost virtual space,
                        not memory: a page takes memory only when it's
                        used, and S0 may be larger than physical memory
                        (its system page table takes a page of memory per
                        128 S0 pages).  A region left out (or given 0)
                        takes its paging default, sized for processes
                        rather than for memory:

                            /P1   5120: the 4096 pages above the user
                                  stack's top, which are inside P1 but
                                  have no pages, and 1024 for the user
                                  stack and P1's expansions
                            /P0   19456: with /P1, a table area of
                                  24576 pages, which is also each new
                                  process's (vax.sysgen.virtualpagecnt)
                            /S0   16384: room for the page tables,
                                  stacks, and PCBs of some 65
                                  processes at once

                        With paging on, /P1 must be at least 4116: the
                        4096 pages above the user stack's top and LINK's
                        default user stack of 20 pages.  A smaller /P1 is
                        refused (P1TOOSMALL).

                        S0 has a minimum size, based on the sizes of the
                        other regions: it holds the page tables for P0 and
                        P1, as well as the privileged modes' stacks.  If S0
                        is too small, or (with paging off) larger than
                        physical memory, or (with paging on) VMINIT's own
                        part of it doesn't fit in physical memory, an
                        error message is displayed.

        /KSP=pages      The size of the kernel stack.  Default 4.
        /ESP=pages      The size of the executive stack.  Default 8.  Its
                        pages are writable only from executive or kernel
                        mode, with a no-access guard page below them.
        /SSP=pages      The size of the supervisor stack.  Default 8.  Its
                        pages are writable only from supervisor mode or a
                        more privileged mode, with a guard page below them.
        /ISP=pages      The size of the interrupt stack.  Default 4.
        /STRINGPOOL=pages
                        The size of the console's string pool.  Default 8.
        /[NO]PAGING     Whether VMS paging is on: working sets, the free
                        and modified page lists, and a page file.  Without
                        either, the vax.sysgen.paging.enable setting
                        decides (see HELP CONFIG KEYS); it is off unless
                        set.  The choice lasts until the next VMINIT.
                        With paging on, each process has a working set (HELP
                        SET WORKING_SET), pages that leave it keep their
                        memory on the free or modified page list and come
                        back without I/O when touched again, and a
                        reclaimer trims working sets when free memory
                        runs low.  When the modified list grows long, the
                        modified page writer writes its pages to the page
                        file (vax.sysgen.paging.file), or a file
                        section's to its file, and a page written out is
                        read back when touched.  Each job's PGFLQUOTA
                        limits the pages it may create.  Section pages
                        page too: a global section's pages are shared
                        among the working sets holding them and leave
                        memory when none does, a page-file section's
                        pages take memory only when touched, and a hard
                        fault reads the neighboring pages of the same
                        file with it (the section's cluster size, or
                        vax.sysgen.pfcdefault).  RUN maps an image
                        rather than copying it: its pages are read from
                        the image file when first touched (the file is
                        held open while the image runs; on a volume,
                        others may only read it), and a writable page
                        becomes the process's own copy, backed by the
                        page file.  A process's address space has only
                        the pages created in it (an access past them is an
                        access violation, but for the user stack, which
                        grows by itself from user mode), and only the
                        parts of its page tables those pages need take
                        memory; the regions share the tables' size (/P0
                        plus /P1 for the console's process,
                        vax.sysgen.virtualpagecnt for the others).  Each
                        page is charged to the job's PGFLQUOTA when it's
                        created, and a creation stops where the quota
                        runs out; a P1 creation the tables can't hold
                        (or a reference that far below the stack) makes
                        no pages at all.  An exception the user stack
                        has no room to report ends the image.  The S0
                        pool's pages take memory only while allocated.
                        SHOW MEMORY shows the counts, and
                        govax's --stats option reports them at exit.

    Example:

        VAX> INITIALIZE/VAX ^D16384
        VAX> VMINIT /P0=16384 /P1=8192 /S0=8192 /KSP=20
